{
  "$comment": "Explicit ledger of existing lint debt, remeasured on unchanged source during the ESLint 10 migration (docs/maintenance/eslint-10-migration.md). `npm run lint` fails on anything not recorded here, on a recorded entry that grows, and on a recorded entry that shrinks without being re-recorded. See scripts/lint-baseline.mjs. Regenerate with `npm run lint:baseline`; raising a count needs a reviewer to agree in the PR.",
  "rationale": {
    "@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any": "The untyped edge of the Python API. Each one is a response body, an SSE event payload or a third-party callback that reaches the UI as `any`. Typing them is a per-endpoint contract change against the backend, not a lint fix, and doing it as one sweep would produce a diff nobody can review. The ratchet's job here is to stop a NEW endpoint from landing untyped; the recorded ones come down endpoint by endpoint.",
    "react-hooks/set-state-in-effect": "Legitimate-looking `reset derived state when the subject changes` and prop-mirroring effects in components with no test coverage. Each rewrite changes when a render happens, so each needs its own regression test first; batching them behind a lint deadline is how a subtle render-order bug ships.",
    "react-hooks/preserve-manual-memoization": "Advice for adopting the React Compiler, not a defect. `babel-plugin-react-compiler` is not in this project's Vite plugin list, so no shipped behaviour depends on these; they become actionable in the change that turns the compiler on.",
    "react-refresh/only-export-components": "`src/context/ApiContext.tsx` only. Every other context module in the UI now splits its handle from its provider, but this one is 3142 lines with ~78 consumers, and the split means relocating the ~1200-line provider in the single most contended file in the repository (36 unmerged branches currently modify it). The cost is a merge conflict for everyone else, and the benefit is hot-reload for one file — so it belongs in a change that owns ApiContext, not in a lint cleanup.",
    "react-hooks/exhaustive-deps": "Inline `eslint-disable` comments predating this cleanup. Each one also opts its component out of the compiler-backed hook analysis, so the suppressed count is a direct measure of the UI the other react-hooks rules cannot see: removing all 43 raises the error count rather than lowering it. Frozen here so that area cannot grow while it is retired component by component.",
    "react-hooks/refs": "Render-phase ref access and existing inline suppressions. Hooks 7.1.1 reports violations that 7.0.1 skipped after an earlier compiler diagnostic. Replacing these latest-value bridges changes render/commit timing and needs consumer-specific tests. The ESLint 10 migration remeasures unchanged source, not additional suppressions; both counts remain frozen. See docs/maintenance/eslint-10-migration.md.",
    "react-hooks/immutability": "Hooks 7.1.1 now reports existing forward references in effects/callbacks and a render-phase mutation through a ref passed to a hook. These are compiler-analysis restrictions, not proof that the callbacks execute before initialization. Source is unchanged in the migration; fixes belong with the affected component's lifecycle tests.",
    "react-hooks/purity": "Three existing render-time Date.now reads used by expiry/countdown displays, newly visible with Hooks 7.1.1. Moving time into state requires an explicit ticking and cleanup contract; the migration freezes these sites rather than changing display timing.",
    "react-hooks/static-components": "ChannelList's existing render-local Wrapper component is newly reported when the compiler continues past earlier diagnostics. Hoisting it and preserving state/props is a separate component change, not part of the lint-tool upgrade.",
    "no-useless-assignment": "Ten existing overwritten initial values detected by the new ESLint 10 recommended rule. They are recorded on unchanged source for this tool-only migration, not permission for new dead assignments; each later cleanup must reduce its per-file count.",
    "preserve-caught-error": "One backend-enablement recovery diagnostic throws a new error inside an outer catch without carrying that caught error as its cause. ESLint 10 newly recommends cause preservation. The diagnostic describes a separate failed readback, so changing its error provenance belongs with the recovery-path tests; this migration records the existing shape without changing runtime behavior."
  },
